Title = {Net carbon balance analysis of urban parks from construction to
   operation: a multi-source data study in Zhengzhou},
Journal = {ECOLOGICAL INDICATORS},
Year = {2025},
Volume = {179},
Month = {OCT},
Abstract = {Amid global climate change and carbon neutrality targets, urban parks
   serve a crucial function in mitigating urban carbon emissions by
   facilitating carbon sequestration and moderating microclimatic
   conditions. Most existing studies on urban parks have focused on carbon
   sequestration potential, whereas relatively little attention has been
   paid to carbon emissions. Consequently, research on the net carbon
   balance of urban parks throughout their life cycle remains limited. This
   study integrates multi-source data, active LiDAR, and field survey data
   to quantify the spatiotemporal patterns of carbon sequestration and
   carbon emissions, and simulates the process of achieving net carbon
   balance in urban parks. Results indicate that the carbon sequestration
   density of urban parks has shown a sustained upward trend, with a
   notable increase in the later stages. Most parks have reached a
   sequestration density of 5.61-7.24 kg center dot CO2-eq center dot m-2
   center dot yr-1. Construction-phase emissions averaged 31.71 kg center
   dot CO2-eq center dot m-2, with 74 \% attributed to building material
   production. Electricity-related emissions during the operational phase
   amounted to 2.02 kg center dot CO2-eq center dot m-2 center dot yr-1.
   Emissions from vegetation maintenance ranged from 0.77 to 1.04 kg center
   dot CO2-eq center dot m-2 center dot yr-1, depending on the intensity of
   management. The average time for parks to achieve carbon neutrality was
   between 9 and 11 years. This study also estimated the net carbon balance
   and applied the Shapley Additive exPlanations (SHAP) analysis to
   evaluate the contributions of different variables to the net carbon
   balance coefficient (NCBC). Among these, the floor area ratio exerted
   the strongest negative effect. These findings provide methodological
   insights and data support for carbon management and low-carbon design in
   urban park planning.},
